<h2>Malware samples</h2><ul>
<li><strong>nfs</strong> (Mirai-derived DDoS bot), SHA-256 <code>0c5486f06620e26e316f4b33f318b0d646d5e1133789331aa2fed012360d1486</code>: A DDoS bot built on Mirai with an encrypted C2 channel. It finds its C2 through a domain and, as a backup, through Ethereum ENS names.</li>
<li><strong>x86_64(2)</strong> (Mirai variant &quot;2.0.4_socks5&quot;), SHA-256 <code>7d9965deae0d3d9834a1299e98ce901329ae071f8744f97746137ea033f100a9</code>: A Mirai DDoS bot. In the sandbox its domain lookup failed, so it used its fallback address. That C2 was live: it accepted the bot and kept the session open, but sent no attack command.</li>
<li><strong>cb</strong> (Nim implant &quot;crong3-agent&quot; (build 3.0.0-929), poses as &quot;sysmon&quot;), SHA-256 <code>a5289abece66fbaf33a6186f315fb6901658e8551c68933e56e65838b95fc3af</code>: A backdoor that stays on the host in many ways and reads its C2 from an Ethereum ENS name and a Solana account. It never uses the normal DNS resolver. The sandbox blocked its connections, so it did not reach a C2.</li>
<li><strong>f0aa…</strong> (Unknown), SHA-256 <code>f0aa83bbbd2c75e2f71ec16029ee5fcfad59f3a8efa30a500b815f0f6c18d987</code>: An unidentified program with no static analysis yet. In the sandbox it looked up a name over DNS over TLS and sent one encrypted message to a server that never answered.</li>
</ul>
